3. `Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1 || (New_Ret >= 0 && New_Ret <= Old_arrListCount));` - This is checking if the previous last index of x in the old list was -1, or if the new return index is within the bounds of the old array list's count after the operation. This essentially asserts that if an element is not in the list, the IndexOf() method returns -1.

4. `Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX < Old_arrListCount || New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);` - This asserts that the new LastIndexOf x is less than the old count of the list or if it's -1, indicating that x might have not existed in the list before the operation.

5. `Debug.Assert(New_Ret == New_arrListLastIndexOfX);` - This asserts that the return value of the IndexOf() method is the same as the New LastIndexOf x, showing that the method returns the index where it found x in the list.

6. `Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == false && New_arrListContainsX == false || Old_arrListContainsX == true && New_arrListContainsX == true);` - This asserts if the Old_arrListContainsX was false and New_arrListContainsX is also false, or if they were both true, indicating that the x is found (if Old_arrListContainsX was true) or not found (if Old_arrListContainsX was false) before and after the operation.
